WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.420
How can a cell make a reaction run uphill without breaking thermodynamics?

2
00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.500
Watch what happens when glucose receives a phosphate.

3
00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:14.940
At cellular concentrations, glucose plus free phosphate favors the starting side.

4
00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.000
Its actual delta ΔG is positive, so the cart rolls backward.

5
00:00:23.883 --> 00:00:26.503
Here is the rule that governs every case.

6
00:00:27.703 --> 00:00:31.923
A reaction runs forward only when its actual delta ΔG is negative.

7
00:00:33.423 --> 00:00:36.723
Enzymes lower activation barriers and speed traffic.

8
00:00:37.883 --> 00:00:39.943
They cannot change the sign of delta ΔG.

9
00:00:41.023 --> 00:00:45.123
The cell must change the chemical journey or the concentrations along it.

10
00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:50.787
In simplest form, the cell ties an uphill reaction to a downhill one.

11
00:00:52.127 --> 00:00:57.327
Hexokinase directly transfers ATP's end phosphoryl to glucose, not through free

12
00:00:57.327 --> 00:01:03.667
phosphate. ATP becomes ADP as glucose becomes glucose-6-phosphate.

13
00:01:04.847 --> 00:01:08.467
This is one chemical reaction, so the free-energy changes add.

14
00:01:09.747 --> 00:01:13.847
Its actual delta ΔG is negative, and the joined carts move forward.

15
00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:19.210
Now predict what happens if a mutant enzyme separates the jobs.

16
00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:22.270
One site hydrolyzes ATP.

17
00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:28.790
Another site tries to attach free phosphate to glucose, with no shared intermediate

18
00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:30.290
or conformational link.

19
00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:34.470
The standard free-energy changes still add on paper.

20
00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:37.410
Will glucose-6-phosphate form?

21
00:01:43.417 --> 00:01:49.837
No. ATP hydrolysis runs downhill, but its free energy leaves as heat.

22
00:01:51.077 --> 00:01:53.117
The glucose cart still rolls backward.

23
00:01:54.597 --> 00:01:56.317
Arithmetic alone is not coupling.

24
00:01:57.977 --> 00:02:02.537
Chemistry must be joined by direct transfer, a shared intermediate, or linked shape

25
00:02:02.537 --> 00:02:03.537
changes.

26
00:02:04.117 --> 00:02:06.197
Cells also change concentrations.

27
00:02:07.617 --> 00:02:12.557
Here, A → B + C has standard delta ΔG°' = +21 kJ/mol.

28
00:02:13.997 --> 00:02:20.677
B → D has ΔG°' = −34 Draining B pulls the first step's actual delta ΔG below zero.

29
00:02:22.037 --> 00:02:25.577
Although the pathway total is minus −13 that is not enough.

30
00:02:26.917 --> 00:02:31.217
Every step carrying forward flux must have negative actual delta ΔG.

31
00:02:31.217 --> 00:02:32.097
B to D has negative actual delta G.

32
00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.240
Take a separate standard example.

33
00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:40.880
Coupling to ATP shifts K from about 0.001 to about 267.

34
00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:46.520
That moves equilibrium toward products by more than 200,000-fold.

35
00:02:47.633 --> 00:02:49.573
That is the answer to our puzzle.

36
00:02:50.993 --> 00:02:55.373
Hexokinase's direct phosphoryl transfer changes the journey, so glucose

37
00:02:55.373 --> 00:02:57.413
phosphorylation can run forward.

38
00:02:58.673 --> 00:03:01.413
A following step may instead keep a product scarce.

39
00:03:02.733 --> 00:03:06.473
The same rule governs ATP and other activated carriers.

40
00:03:07.873 --> 00:03:12.633
Uphill chemistry runs only when coupling makes its actual delta ΔG negative.
